"Aristotle pointed out that is not is ambiguous.": "14b3ce3902b2",
"Aristotles answer has lasted longest. He pointed out that is not is ambiguous. An acorn is not an oak, but it is not simply nothing either. It is potentially an oak. Change is the passage from being something potentially to being it actually, and it always has three terms: something that persists, a condition it lacked, and a condition it acquires. When a green leaf turns red, the leaf remains. Change, on this analysis, requires sameness. Parmenides was right that nothing comes from nothing, and wrong to think that change needs it to.": "b08823b9fc6b",
"Around 500 BCE, at opposite ends of the Greek world, two thinkers reached conclusions that could hardly be further apart. Heraclitus of Ephesus, on the coast of what is now Turkey, taught that the world is a process, like a fire or a river, in which nothing stays as it is. Parmenides of Elea, in southern Italy, argued that change is impossible and that what truly exists is one, whole, and motionless. Neither could be dismissed. Heraclitus had the evidence of the senses on his side, and Parmenides had an argument that nobody could answer. Philosophy after them had to explain how both could be so persuasive.": "573835077e51",
"At about the time Heraclitus was writing, Confucius stood beside a river and said: What passes away is like this, is it not? It does not cease, day or night (逝者如斯夫，不舍晝夜). The Analects gives no explanation, and readers have supplied two. One hears a sigh over time and mortality, the feeling that everything is carried off. The other, which became standard among the Neo-Confucians, hears admiration. The stream never rests, and the person who is learning should be like it. Both readings find in the river what Heraclitus found there. Heraclitus made a theory of the world from it, and the Chinese tradition took it as a lesson in how to live.": "c0ee9574d45e",
"For thou canst not know what is not—that is impossible—nor utter it; for it is the same thing that can be thought and that can be.": "0ba1f2643b21",
"From this the rest follows with terrible ease.": "1d0c68ba1f47",
"From this the rest follows with terrible ease. For a thing to come into being, it would have to come from what is not, and there is no such thing. For a thing to change, it would have to become what it is not. For there to be two things, there would have to be something between them that is not either of them. So what is is uncreated and indestructible, single, continuous, and unmoving. The changing world of the senses is an appearance that mortals have agreed to name. Parmenides pupil Zeno wrote the paradoxes of lesson A4 in order to show that those who laughed at this conclusion were worse off than his master.": "3af8fb84e146",
"Heraclitus therefore taught more than flux. He taught that the flux is ordered. The world is an ever-living Fire, with measures of it kindling, and measures going out, and the word to notice is measures. Fire is his image for a thing that exists only by consuming and being fed, and a steady flame is a balance between the two. He called the principle of this balance the logos, a word that means both account and proportion, and he complained that people live as though they each had a private understanding when the logos is common to all. He saw the same balance in opposites. The way up and the way down is one and the same. Day and night, waking and sleeping, the bows wood pulling one way and its string the other: in each case two contrary things turn out to be one process seen from two sides. The hidden attunement, he said, is better than the open.": "34a098e0c085",
"Heraclitus wrote in short, dense sayings, of which about a hundred and thirty survive as quotations in later authors. The most famous concern a river. You cannot step twice into the same rivers; for fresh waters are ever flowing in upon you. And, more strangely: We step and do not step into the same rivers; we are and are not. Plato reported the doctrine in the form that became proverbial, that all things are in motion and nothing at rest, and that you cannot go into the same water twice.": "7444e6555e5b",
"Heraclitus wrote in short, dense sayings.": "3c1eb6e5a166",
"If you take the first picture as basic, you have to explain why we seem to experience change.": "1815210df170",
"It is easy to hear this as a denial that anything is ever the same, and one of his followers took it that way. Cratylus, according to Aristotle, criticized his master for saying that one cannot step twice into the same river, for he thought one could not do it even once, and in the end he gave up speaking and only moved his finger. But look at the first saying again. It speaks of the same rivers. Heraclitus does not deny that there is one river, the Cayster, into which the Ephesians stepped every day. His point is about what makes it one. Dam it, so that the water stops being replaced, and you no longer have a river. The sameness of the river consists in the exchange of its waters.": "7f307b8f1d1f",
"Neither side has gone away. Modern physics has a Parmenidean face. Its deepest laws are conservation laws, statements that some quantity stays the same through every change, and the theory of relativity is often read as describing a four-dimensional block in which past, present, and future are equally real and nothing flows at all. It also has a Heraclitean face. A flame, a whirlpool, a living cell, and a human body are stable patterns in a stream of matter and energy, and they exist only as long as the stream continues. If you take the first picture as basic you have to explain why we seem to experience change. If you take the second as basic you have to explain what it is that stays the same. The river is where the two questions meet, and that is why the saying is still quoted after twenty-five centuries.": "e480bc45cd2b",
"Neither thinker could be dismissed.": "087d3be4b360",
"On impermanence the Buddha and Heraclitus say nearly the same thing, and they use the same image. All conditioned things are impermanent (sabbe saṅkhārā aniccā), says the Dhammapada. A discourse in the Aṅguttara Nikāya compares human life to a mountain river, flowing far and fast, of which there is no moment, no instant, no second when it turns back (7.74). Later Buddhist philosophers took the thought to its limit and held that each thing lasts only a moment, so that what we call one object is a rapid series of similar ones. That is very close to the position of Cratylus.": "923cac9d73ac",
"One hears the feeling that everything is carried off.": "6d473eac1de4",
"Parmenides set out his view in a poem, in which a goddess reveals to him the only two ways of search that can be thought of. The first is that It is, and that it is impossible for it not to be. The second is that It is not. The second way, she says, cannot be followed at all: For thou canst not know what is not—that is impossible—nor utter it; for it is the same thing that can be thought and that can be. To think is to think of something. What is not, is nothing, and nothing is not there to be thought of.": "d92f869353fe",
"Plato reported the doctrine in the form that became proverbial.": "46a8a2eaf2f6",
"The differences are instructive. Heraclituss flux has something permanent in it. The logos does not change, and the fire is ever-living. Early Buddhism names no such underlying stuff, and its regularity, dependent origination, is a pattern among events and is not treated as a thing in its own right. The two also disagree about what the insight is for. To Heraclitus the fire of the world is something to be understood and admired. In the Fire Sermon the Buddha tells his monks that all is burning, the eye, the ear, the mind, burning with greed, hatred, and delusion, and the point of seeing this is to let the fire go out (Saṃyutta Nikāya 35.28). The word nirvāṇa means exactly that. And where the Greek debate had two sides, the Buddhist tradition counts three. Against Parmenides it denies that anything is permanent. Against the extreme of Cratylus it affirms that conditions reliably give rise to their effects. Its own position is the middle one, that there is continuity and no identity (Dharma and the West, D5).": "f0bdf5b41b64",
"The next two centuries of Greek philosophy can be read as a series of attempts to grant Parmenides his premise and still keep the river. The atomists accepted that what truly exists never comes into being or perishes, and said that there are countless such things, atoms, whose rearrangement in empty space is what we call change. Plato divided the world in two. The Forms are as Parmenides described, eternal and unchanging, and they are what knowledge is about. The world we perceive is as Heraclitus described, and of it we have only opinion.": "8400edffe9e5",
"The sameness of the river consists in the exchange of its waters.": "9f2816cb12f3",
"They tried to grant Parmenides his premise and still keep the river.": "f7ce9a37f7dc",
